SQL injection:
    What is SQL Injection?
    A vulnerability where untrusted input is treated as part of an SQL command, allowing an attacker to change the query’s behavior (read/modify/delete data, bypass auth).
    Vulnerable example (JDBC, DO NOT DO THIS):
    // BAD: concatenating user input into SQL
    String username = request.getParameter("user");
    String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '" + username + "'";
    Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();
    ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(sql);
    If attacker sends user = ' OR '1'='1, the query becomes:
    SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '' OR '1'='1'

    Safe fix — use parameterized queries (PreparedStatement):
    String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?";
    PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql);
    ps.setString(1, username);
    ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery();

    Prepared statements keep data separate from code, preventing injection.

    Quick prevention checklist
    	•	Use parameterized queries / prepared statements (primary defense).
    	•	Use ORMs (e.g., JPA/Hibernate) correctly — avoid native queries with concatenation.
    	•	Apply input validation & whitelisting (only allow expected formats).
    	•	Use least-privilege DB accounts (avoid giving app users DDL or admin rights).
    	•	Enable logging, monitoring, WAF, and perform regular security testing (SAST/DAST, pen-tests).

    In one sentence:
    SQL injection happens when user input is mixed into SQL; prevent it by separating code from data (prepared statements), validating input, and restricting DB privileges.

Dependency injection:
    object’s dependencies are provided (injected) from the outside rather than the object creating
    It’s a form of Inversion of Control (IoC) — the object asks for what it needs instead of constructing it.